    "q-163685": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163685",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-163685-a",
